            [
                'title' => 'Farm Safety Systems and Emergency Preparedness',
                'summary' => 'Plan for the day nothing goes right.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A well-run farm plans for the day nothing goes right, and farm safety systems and emergency preparedness is the discipline of planning for it.\n\nStart with the hazards, written down. Every farm has them: machinery, heights, chemicals, heat, and electricity. The manager who lists the hazards, and assigns a control to each, has turned a worry into a managed risk, and the managed risk is the one that rarely fires.\n\nBuild the emergency plan. Who calls for help, where the first-aid kit is, how to reach the hospital, and what to do at a fire, a injury, or a chemical spill. The manager who writes the plan, posts it, and drills it, gives the crew the reflexes that save lives, and the drilled response is the one that works under pressure.\n\nKeep the records. Incidents, near-misses, and the fixes, logged and reviewed, are the farm's safety history. The manager who reads that history, and acts on it, prevents the repeat, and the farm that learns from its near-misses has fewer of them.\n\nFinally, make safety everyone's job. The crew that briefs, watches, and corrects each other is the crew that stays safe, and the manager who builds that culture builds a farm where people go home healthy, and healthy people are the farm's most valuable asset.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => "The Operations Manager's Year",
                'summary' => 'The calendar that runs the farm.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The operations manager runs the farm on a calendar, and the operations manager's year is the discipline of planning the season before it starts.\n\nStart with the farm's rhythm. The planting, the fertilising, the pest windows, the harvest, and the market, each has its time. The manager who maps the year, and plans the work around it, is the manager whose farm flows with the seasons, rather than chasing them.\n\nBuild the calendar around the money. The input purchases, the labour peaks, and the sales, timed to the farm's cash, keep the farm solvent. The manager who plans the spending to the income, and the income to the season, never faces a cash squeeze they did not see coming.\n\nReserve the review. The weekly walk, the monthly accounts, and the seasonal review, kept in the calendar, turn the plan into a managed business. The manager who reviews, and adjusts, is the manager who improves, and improvement is the farm's compounding return.\n\nFinally, share the calendar. A farm plan the crew can see is a farm the crew can run. The manager who publishes the year, and follows it, runs a farm that does not depend on their constant presence, and that independence is the mark of a real operations manager.",
            ],
