    9 => [
        [
            'title' => 'Record Keeping for Regulatory Compliance',
            'summary' => 'Keep the records the regulator wants.',
                'duration' => 60,
            'content' => "A provider's compliance is only as good as its records, and record keeping for regulatory compliance is the discipline of keeping the records the regulator wants.\n\nStart with the required records. The purchase invoices, the supplier details, the batch numbers, the sales, and the licences, kept current and retrievable. The provider who keeps the required records, and can produce them on request, is the provider the regulator passes, and the passing provider keeps the accreditation.\n\nKeep them organised and backed up. A record that cannot be found is a record that does not exist, so the provider who organises the records, and backs them up, protects the business from the lost-record crisis. The organised provider is the provider that survives an inspection.\n\nKeep the traceability. Every product, from the supplier to the shelf, traced by batch and date. The provider who traces the product can answer the recall in minutes, and the fast recall is the one that protects the farmer and the provider's name.\n\nFinally, review the records regularly. The gaps, the expired licences, and the missing documents, found and fixed before the audit. The provider who reviews the records, and closes the gaps, is the provider whose compliance is real, and real compliance is the provider's licence to trade.",
